## If Inside If: Nesting

You can put an `if` statement inside another `if` block:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let is_raining = true;
  let has_umbrella = false;

  if is_raining {
    io.println("It's raining!");
    if has_umbrella {
      io.println("You have an umbrella — you'll stay dry.");
    } else {
      io.println("No umbrella — you'll get wet!");
    }
  } else {
    io.println("No rain today!");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

### When the Inner If Runs

The inner `if` is only even CONSIDERED when the outer `if` condition is true:

```xiom
if logged_in {
  // We're inside the outer if — user IS logged in
  if is_admin {
    io.println("Welcome, admin!");
  } else {
    io.println("Welcome, user!");
  }
}
// If not logged in, we skip everything above
```

### Nested vs Combined

You could write the same logic two ways:

```xiom
// Nested — shows hierarchy ("only check admin if logged in")
if logged_in {
  if is_admin {
    io.println("Admin dashboard");
  }
}

// Combined — flat condition ("check both at once")
if logged_in && is_admin {
  io.println("Admin dashboard");
}
```

Both work. Use nested when the inner condition logically depends on the outer one. Use `&&` when they're independent but both required.

### Don't Go Too Deep

Nesting more than 2-3 levels deep makes code hard to follow:

```xiom
// Too deep — hard to read!
if a {
  if b {
    if c {
      if d {
        // what was I checking again?
      }
    }
  }
}
```

If you're nesting deeply, consider using `&&`, early returns, or restructuring your logic.

